                <pb facs="0582" n="576" sameAs="frus:frus1894app1/pg576"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Herbert to Mr. Foster.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >British Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1892-10-06"><hi rendition="#italic">October 6,
                        1892</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inform you that I have
                    received a telegram from the Earl of Rosebery stating that the German
                    Government, in view of the disturbed state of affairs in Samoa, proposes to send
                    one or two men-of-war there to protect life and property. His lordship sees no
                    objection to this proposal and as, in his opinion, it is desirable to maintain
                    the strict concert of the treaty powers, he is disposed to recommend
                    acquiescence in it.</p>
                <p>I should be very glad to learn the views of your Government in regard to this
                    matter.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Michael H.
                    Herbert.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
